n, rapid iteration, and leveraging the technical backend architecture already scaffolded for your food app project.\n\n---\n\n### Phase 1: Ideation & Validation (Weeks 1-4)\n*Goal: Define the problem-solution fit before writing heavy code.*\n1.  **Problem Discovery:** Conduct 50+ interviews with potential users. Identify a \"bleeding neck\" problem rather than a \"nice-to-have\" feature.\n2.  **Competitor Mapping:** Analyze existing AI incumbents. Identify the \"unbundling\" opportunity where your AI can perform a specific task 10x faster or cheaper.\n3.  **Technical Feasibility Assessment:** Evaluate if your AI solution requires custom model training (compute heavy) or API-based integration (e.g., OpenAI/Anthropic/Claude API).\n4.  **Value Proposition Canvas:** Map the specific pains of your user persona to the gains provided by your AI model.\n\n### Phase 2: MVP Development (Weeks 5-12)\n*Goal: Build the \"Minimum Lovable Product\" focusing on core AI utility.*\n1.  **Infrastructure Setup:** Utilize the existing **NEXUS AI monorepo structure** (Microservices: `order`, `restaurant`, `payment`, `auth`) to handle non-AI logic, keeping the core AI service modular.\n2.  **Data Pipeline Creation:** Build the data ingestion layer to feed the AI model (training or RAG implementation).\n3.  **Lean Frontend/UX:** Focus on a high-utility interface. If your startup is AI-heavy, the UI should prioritize clear input/output and feedback loops for model RLHF (Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback).\n4.  **Internal Alpha:** The 4-person team tests the product for edge cases.\n\n### Phase 3: Go-to-Market (Weeks 13-16)\n*Goal: Secure initial traction and user data.*\n1.  **Beta Launch:** Release to a closed group of 100 users. Focus on high-intent communities (Reddit, Discord, Niche Forums).\n2.  **Telemetry & Analytics:** Implement user tracking. Monitor \"AI hallucination\" rates and user retention metrics.\n3.  **Feedback Loop Implementation:** Build a direct channel where users can report incorrect AI outputs. Use this to update your prompt engineering or fine-tuning datasets.\n4.  **Content Marketing:** Publish technical \"Build in Public\" articles detailing how you used the NEXUS architecture to solve the problem.\n\n### Phase 4: Fundraising (Weeks 17-20)\n*Goal: Secure Pre-Seed or Seed capital based on traction.*\n1.  **Data Room Preparation:** Organize your pitch deck, team bios (highlighting your 4-founder synergy), and the traction metrics gained in Phase 3.\n2.  **Investor Outreach:** Target \"Operator-Investors\" who understand AI technical debt and infrastructure.\n3.  **Product Roadmap Presentation:** Present a 12-month vision, specifically how you will scale the AI architecture beyond the initial MVP.\n\n### Phase 5: Scaling & Optimization (Weeks 21+)\n*Goal: Optimize for cost and performance.*\n1.  **Compute Optimization:** Transition from expensive API calls to optimized open-source models (e.g., Llama 3, Mistral) on private cloud clusters.\n2.  **Feature Expansion:** Develop new microservices within the existing monorepo to expand the product ecosystem.\n3.  **Team Specialization:** Transition from \"Generalist Founders\" to functional leads (e.g., Head of Product, Head of AI Research, Head of Growth, Head of Engineering).\n\n---\n\n### Potential Failure Points & Mitigation\n\n| Failure Point | Impact | Mitigation Strategy |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Founder Conflict** | High | Establish a \"Founders' Agreement\" early. Define clear decision-making authority for each of the 4 roles. |\n| **Model Hallucinations** | High | Implement strict output validation layers and human-in-the-loop (HITL) checkpoints. |\n| **\"Over-Engineering\"** | Medium | Use the NEXUS scaffold as-is. Don't build custom infrastructure if a managed service suffices for now. |\n| **Burnout** | High | Set explicit \"sprint\" and \"recovery\" weeks. Ensure team roles rotate slightly to maintain perspective. |\n\n**NEXUS AI Recommendation:** Given your experience with the microservices monorepo, ensure that your AI service remains **decoupled** from your database layer. This allows you to hot-swap models without rebuilding the entire application structure.", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": null, "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null, "extra_content": {"google": {"thought_signature": "EjQKMgG+Pvb7BxNsK4CluDpBvfXB4wSmIL4/hymQShJSXoky0VPzsE36ql6Osak05WSCdQrv"}}}}], "created": 1774247524, "model": "gemini-3.1-flash-lite-preview", "object": "chat.completion", "service_tier": null, "system_fingerprint": null, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1039, "prompt_tokens": 732, "total_tokens": 1771, "completion_tokens_details": null, "prompt_tokens_details": null}}, "prompt_tokens": 732, "completion_tokens": 1039, "agent_id": null}
